So far, while Pex has found several bugs after testing this code, it seems that it only runs through the Add method in the ArrayList contract tests. I've tried to replicate the pattern of adding other methods to the contract tests but Pex keeps failing. 
Question: How do I incorporate all other ArrayList methods in their respective contracts?

A: You need to use the [PexMethod] and [PexExplore] attributes to mark methods for Pex testing. The [PexMethod] attribute tells Pex to run the method in the presence of the specified arguments, while the [PexExplore] attribute tells Pex to generate a test run with every possible argument combination.

Let's consider just an example. Let's say you have an ArrayList and you have a method AddRange, it should be like this:

```csharp
    [PexMethod]
    public void PUT_AddRangeContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, ArrayList AddRangeList)
    {
        // Your code here...
    }
```

Similarly, you may have to mark other methods with these attributes. 

Also, remember that Pex tests may timeout or fail with big number of combinations. Make sure that your code under test does not contain infinite loops or very heavy computations, especially if you include [PexExplore]. If you must test big permutations consider using the [PexLarge] attribute or reduce the amount of combinations you test with the [PexSmall] attribute.

Lastly, make sure that your ArrayList implementation respects all the contract conditions imposed by the Pex framework. This may necessitate you adding Pex annotations to various array-related methods and properties of your ArrayList implementation. An example could be:

```csharp
    [PexContract]
    public int IndexOf(T item)
    {
        // Your code here...
    }
```

You might want to test other contracts as well, and that's where you can use a combination of [PexMethod] for running specific permutations, and [PexExplore] for generating a large number of random permutations. Remember, testing all permutations can be time-consuming, particularly with larger permutations.

Also, make sure you understand the warnings and failures before fixing them: they often give clues to--------------------		AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(New_Ret == true ? New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 : true);

        }
    }      
