```PexProblemReport.xml``` file content:

```xml
<PexProblemReport>
  <ReportedProblems>
    <Problem Test="PUT_ContainssContract.plex10">
      <Violation Message="The condition '(int) (arrList[index]) == Old_x' is always true. This might be due to under-constraining of the arguments. Check that the 'Contains' method works correctly and the arguments are constrained accordingly."/>
    </Problem>
  </ReportedProblems>
</PexProblemReport>
```
It seems like problem is caused by IndexOf methods of ArrayList returning -1. Is that possible?

A: It is not possible. IndexOf method of ArrayList class will return -1 if the item does not exist in the ArrayList. However, the Get method can throw an exception if the index is out of bounds. That's why in your contract test, you are observing (int)(arrList[index]). Assuming the size of the list is not changing and the indices are valid, so it should work fine. But, in practice, it will actually throw an IndexOutOfRangeException in the test run.

Please note that the contracts we defined for these methods are based on the assumptions that the size of the list is not changing and the indices are valid, which is not necessarily true during testing. Since the IndexOf, LastIndexOf and Contains methods are expected to return -1, 0 or positive value, we can't specify the return type in contract tests to be int which is correct but could cause testing problem.

So the contract test for Get method in our case should exclude IndexOf of ArrayList.

For your concern, if you don't want to see this exception, you can modify your contract test to only run if the index is within bounds. Here is an example:

```CSharp
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, [PexPositiveValidValues(0, 1, int.MaxValue)] int index)
{
  AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count);
  //...
}
```

This way we ensure index is always valid and the count of the list hasn't changed.

Hope this helps.

A: You should add the `[PexPositiveValidValues]` attribute to your index, ensuring that the index passed into the `GET` method is non-negative and is within the valid index range for the list.

Your `PUT_GetContract` method should look like this:

```CSharp
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, [PexPositiveValidValues(0, 1, int.MaxValue)] int index)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count);

    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int Old_index = index;

    int New_Ret = (int)(arrList[index]);

    int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    bool New_containsNewRet = arrList.Contains(New_Ret);

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_containsNewRet", New_containsNewRet);
}
```

This way the framework will test the method with indices 0, 1 and `max int value`, providing a wide range of input and ensuring that exception is not thrown.

The framework should be able to understand that even if the value at index `index` is 0, it can't be compared with `Old_x` (from your other tests), as `Old_x` could be anything from the list. Therefore this is why IndexOf should return -1 instead of throwing exception.

So now if your ArrayList class works correctly, your tests will not throw any exceptions and will check for the correctness of `GET` method in the ArrayList class.

Keep in mind this way if the test cases run it will be slower compared to your previous tests--------------------